# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Part 1: front matter, chapters 1-10."""

BLOCKS = [
    ("h1", "How to use this book"),
    ("p", "This book is written for one person: an Indian student (or a parent paying the bills) working out how to study in the UK in 2027 without losing money to a rule they did not know about. Every number here was checked against an official UK government page in November 2026, and every chapter ends with the thing to actually do."),
    ("p", "Three things make the 2027 cycle different from the one your cousin did in 2023. The money you must show went up twice, and rises again for applications made on or after 30 November 2026. The Graduate Route - the visa that lets you stay and work after your course - drops from two years to 18 months for applications made on or after 1 January 2027, with a possible exception for graduates of certain universities that the government has announced but not yet defined. And because fewer students are coming, universities are discounting harder than they have in a decade, which means the sticker price on a course page is now a starting point rather than a price."),
    ("p", "So this is not a brochure. It is a working manual: what the rules say, what they cost in rupees, what gets applications refused, and what to do in the order the Home Office expects you to do it."),
    ("h2", "How the chapters are organised"),
    ("b", [
        "Chapters 1 to 4 are the decision. Read them before you pay a single rupee of deposit. They contain the arithmetic most consultancies never show you.",
        "Chapters 5 to 7 are the choice: course, university, intake. This is where the 18-month versus two-year question actually gets decided, months before you apply for a visa.",
        "Chapters 8 to 11 are the money rules - the part that refuses more Indian applications than anything else. Work through them with a calendar open.",
        "Chapters 12 to 19 are the application itself, including the interview and the ten refusal reasons.",
        "Chapters 20 to 26 are after you land: your eVisa, your first fortnight, your work limits, the Graduate Route, and the switch to a work visa.",
        "Chapters 27 and 28 are money coming back: scholarships, and how to ask a university for more.",
        "Chapters 29 to 31 are the worksheets, glossary and sources. Print them.",
    ]),
    ("h2", "A note on currency"),
    ("p", "Fees and government charges are in pounds, because that is how you will pay them. Rupee conversions use the RBI reference rate of 17 November 2026: Rs 123.46 to the pound, rounded to Rs 123.5 in the worked examples. The pound has moved between roughly Rs 123 and Rs 128 in the past month, so a 4 per cent swing on a Rs 35 lakh budget is about Rs 1.4 lakh. Check the rate on the day you transfer, and never convert a large sum at an airport or through a credit card."),
    ("h2", "What this book is not"),
    ("p", "It is not legal advice, and it is not a substitute for the Home Office pages it cites. Immigration rules change - sometimes with three weeks' notice, as the November 2026 funds uplift shows. Where a rule is still being written (the high-compliance university exception is the big one), this book says so plainly instead of guessing. Always confirm the current figure on gov.uk before you apply, and treat any agent who tells you a number without showing you the page as a risk to your money."),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 1 ----------------
    ("h1", "1. The five dates that decide your money"),
    ("p", "Visa rules are decided by the date you apply, not the date you start your course, not the date your CAS was issued, and not the date you were promised something by an agent. Five dates in the 2026-27 cycle carry real money. Put them in your phone now."),
    ("t", (
        ["Date", "What changes", "What it costs you if you ignore it"],
        [
            ["30 Nov 2026", "Student visa maintenance rises to GBP 1,570 a month in London and GBP 1,203 outside London (from GBP 1,529 and GBP 1,171). The accommodation deposit you can offset rises to GBP 1,570.", "About Rs 45,600 more to show in London, Rs 35,600 outside, if you apply on or after this date. An application that shows the old amount is refused."],
            ["31 Dec 2026", "Last day to apply for a Graduate visa and get two years instead of 18 months.", "Six months of UK work permission - the single most valuable thing you will lose by being slow."],
            ["1 Jan 2027", "Graduate Route defaults to 18 months for bachelor's and master's graduates. PhD graduates keep three years.", "As above. Applies to the application date, so a student who finishes in December 2026 and applies in January 2027 gets 18 months."],
            ["Announced, not dated", "2026 Autumn Budget: two years for graduates of 'high compliance universities where there are strong labour market outcomes'. Eligibility and transitional arrangements were still undefined when this book was written.", "Nothing yet - but it changes which university you should pick. See Chapter 6."],
            ["Your application date", "Every other figure in this book is fixed to it: the 28-day clock, the 31-day evidence window, the fee you pay.", "A correctly funded application sent three days late is a refusal, not a delay."],
        ],
        [0.15, 0.47, 0.38],
    )),
    ("h2", "The rule that surprises people: rules attach to the application date"),
    ("p", "When the Home Office changes a requirement, the change applies to applications made on or after the commencement date. Applications already made are decided under the rules in force the day before. The September 2026 statement of changes (HC 584) says exactly this about the funds uplift: it takes effect on 30 November 2026, and an application made before that date is decided under the rules in force on 29 November 2026."),
    ("p", "Two practical consequences. First, if you are ready and your money has been sitting still for 28 days, applying a week before a known uplift is legitimate and saves real money. Second, do not try to game a change you have not read. The same paragraph that protects an early applicant also means that if your application is refused and you reapply after the change, the new, higher figure applies to the reapplication."),
    ("call", ("The 28-day clock is not 28 days of your choosing", "The 28 days are counted backwards from the closing balance on your most recent bank statement. That 28-day period must end no more than 31 days before you apply. So the money has to be in the account, untouched and visible, across a window you must plan backwards from your application date - not forwards from today.")),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 2 ----------------
    ("h1", "2. What the UK actually costs in 2027"),
    ("p", "Consultancy quotes usually show tuition and stop. The Home Office charges, the deposit you cannot get back, the IHS, the flight, the first month's rent before you have a UK bank account - these are the numbers that decide whether your plan survives contact with reality. Here is the full stack, using a one-year master's as the model because that is what most Indian students take."),
    ("h2", "The fixed government charges"),
    ("t", (
        ["Charge", "Amount", "In rupees", "Notes"],
        [
            ["Student visa application", "GBP 558", "Rs 68,900", "Per person, including each dependant. Fee unchanged at the 8 October 2026 fee update."],
            ["Immigration Health Surcharge", "GBP 776 per year", "Rs 95,800 per year", "Student rate. A 12-month master's usually attracts two years of IHS because the visa runs past the course."],
            ["TB test at an approved clinic", "Clinic fee, varies", "Usually Rs 2,000-4,000", "Certificate valid six months from the x-ray date. India is on the list."],
            ["Biometrics / visa application centre", "Included in the fee", "-", "You may pay extra for priority or added services if offered."],
            ["Graduate visa (later)", "GBP 937", "Rs 115,700", "Plus IHS at GBP 1,035 per year for this route - roughly GBP 1,552 for 18 months."],
        ],
        [0.30, 0.18, 0.20, 0.32],
    )),
    ("h2", "A worked total: one-year master's, outside London"),
    ("p", "Assume a taught master's at GBP 22,000, a GBP 4,000 deposit already paid, and a city outside London. These are planning figures, not quotes - your CAS is the only number that matters for tuition."),
    ("t", (
        ["Item", "GBP", "Rs at 123.5"],
        [
            ["Tuition, one year", "22,000", "27,17,000"],
            ["Living costs you must show (9 months x GBP 1,203, from 30 Nov 2026)", "10,827", "13,37,100"],
            ["Student visa fee", "558", "68,900"],
            ["IHS, two years at student rate", "1,552", "1,91,700"],
            ["TB test and biometrics extras", "about 40", "about 4,900"],
            ["Flight, one way, booked early", "about 550", "about 67,900"],
            ["Setup: SIM, bedding, deposit top-ups, transport", "about 800", "about 98,800"],
            ["Total cash out in year one", "about 36,327", "about 44,86,400"],
        ],
        [0.60, 0.18, 0.22],
    )),
    ("p", "Note what that total is and is not. The GBP 10,827 is not a fee - it is money you keep and spend on rent and food. But it has to exist, in a bank account, on a specific date, before you get a visa. That is the difference between a student who plans and a student who is refused for being GBP 400 short."),
    ("p", "London adds roughly GBP 3,300 to the maintenance figure alone (GBP 14,130 against GBP 10,827), and the real rent gap is wider than the government's formula admits. Chapter 4 deals with that."),
    ("h2", "What people forget"),
    ("b", [
        "The deposit is not the fee. Most universities ask for GBP 3,000-5,000 to issue a CAS, and several keep it if your visa is refused for deception or if you withdraw late. Read the refund clause before you pay.",
        "The IHS is charged on visa length, not course length. A 12-month course usually gets a visa running to about 16 months after the start, which often rounds up to two IHS years.",
        "You cannot pay tuition from the maintenance money and still meet the maintenance requirement. The Home Office wants the tuition (minus what you have already paid) plus the living-cost figure, both visible.",
        "Forex mark-up is a real cost. A Rs 25 lakh transfer through a bank card can lose 2-3 per cent against a specialist transfer at the RBI cross-rate.",
    ]),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 3 ----------------
    ("h1", "3. Is it still worth it? The arithmetic, not the dream"),
    ("p", "The honest answer is: for some courses and some people, yes, clearly. For others, the numbers stopped working when the Graduate Route was cut and the pound stayed above Rs 120. This chapter does the sum that a consultancy will not do for you, because the answer sometimes is 'do not go'."),
    ("h2", "The three variables"),
    ("p", "Everything reduces to three numbers. Total cost of the degree in rupees. The salary you can realistically get in the UK, in the job you can realistically get, in the months your visa allows. And what you would have earned in India in the same period. If you cannot name all three, you are not ready to spend Rs 40 lakh."),
    ("t", (
        ["Scenario", "Cost (Rs)", "UK salary assumption", "Break-even, rough"],
        [
            ["One-year master's, non-London, mid-ranked university", "38-45 lakh", "GBP 30,000-34,000 starting in a sponsored role", "3-4 years of UK savings, if you get sponsorship within your visa window"],
            ["One-year master's, London, Russell Group", "52-62 lakh", "GBP 34,000-40,000 in finance, tech, consulting", "4-5 years, faster if the employer sponsors early"],
            ["Two-year master's with placement", "60-75 lakh", "Higher placement conversion, GBP 30,000+", "4-6 years, but the placement year cuts the risk"],
            ["One-year master's in a field with weak UK demand", "38-45 lakh", "Return to India on an Indian salary", "Often never - the degree becomes a consumption good"],
        ],
        [0.32, 0.16, 0.26, 0.26],
    )),
    ("p", "Those ranges are planning assumptions, built from published fee bands and the salary thresholds in Chapter 24 - they are not promises and not statistics. Substitute your own offer letter the moment you have one."),
    ("h2", "The three questions that predict the outcome"),
    ("n", [
        "Does this occupation appear on the Skilled Worker eligible list, and does a UK employer in it actually sponsor? Ask a specific employer name, not 'the sector'.",
        "Can you pass the new entrant salary floor of GBP 33,400, or 70 per cent of the going rate for the role, whichever the rules require for that job? If the realistic starting salary in your field is GBP 26,000, the visa maths does not work no matter how good the course is.",
        "Are you willing to be in the UK in month 15 with a job that does not sponsor? Because that is the default outcome for a share of graduates, and the 18-month route shortens the runway.",
    ]),
    ("h2", "When the UK is the wrong answer"),
    ("p", "If your plan depends on staying in the UK indefinitely, and your target occupation pays below the Skilled Worker floor at entry, the UK is an expensive detour. If your plan is a specific course with a specific industry link - a conversion master's in computer science at a university with a placement pipeline, a niche LLM, a public health degree with an NHS affiliation - the UK still buys you something India cannot sell you at that price."),
    ("p", "And if your real goal is a credential plus two years of international work, note that the competition for that is now explicitly cheaper elsewhere: Germany's public universities charge little or no tuition, and Canada and Australia have their own post-study routes with different arithmetic. This book is not neutral on that point - it is neutral only about the facts."),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 4 ----------------
    ("h1", "4. London, or everywhere else"),
    ("p", "The government draws one line and it is expensive. London means the City of London and the 32 London boroughs. Everything else - Manchester, Birmingham, Leeds, Glasgow, Cardiff, Bristol, Newcastle, Coventry - is 'outside London' for visa purposes, even when it feels like a big city with big-city rents."),
    ("t", (
        ["", "London", "Outside London"],
        [
            ["Maintenance per month, applications on or after 30 Nov 2026", "GBP 1,570", "GBP 1,203"],
            ["Maximum over 9 months", "GBP 14,130", "GBP 10,827"],
            ["Each dependant, per month", "GBP 845", "GBP 680"],
            ["Accommodation deposit you may offset", "up to GBP 1,570", "up to GBP 1,570"],
            ["Typical share of a flat, per month, planning range", "GBP 750-1,100", "GBP 450-700"],
            ["Typical university hall room, per month, planning range", "GBP 700-1,000", "GBP 450-750"],
        ],
        [0.50, 0.25, 0.25],
    )),
    ("p", "The maintenance figure is a floor set for the whole country. In London it is a tight budget; outside London it is workable if you cook. The rent ranges above are planning estimates from advertised student accommodation, not official statistics - check the specific hall or letting agent before you commit, and remember that a 51-week tenancy costs more than a 44-week one even at the same weekly rent."),
    ("h2", "What London buys"),
    ("b", [
        "Density of employers. If your target is consulting, finance, law, media or a startup, most interviews happen in one city and being there removes a train fare and a scheduling excuse.",
        "Part-time work at higher hourly rates, and more of it. The 20-hour cap applies everywhere, but the London hourly rate is higher.",
        "A larger Indian student community, which matters more than people admit in month one.",
    ]),
    ("h2", "What London costs beyond rent"),
    ("p", "Transport. A monthly student travelcard in London is a four-figure annual cost in pounds that no other UK city replicates; most other cities are walkable or cyclable from student areas. Add higher food, higher social spend, and the tendency to live further out to save rent, which then adds 45 minutes each way and kills the part-time job you were counting on."),
    ("call", ("The break-even question", "If the London premium over a non-London city is roughly GBP 6,000-9,000 a year in real spending, does the London version of your course raise your expected starting salary by more than that? If the answer is 'maybe', pick the cheaper city and spend the difference on being able to take an unpaid-adjacent internship.")),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 5 ----------------
    ("h1", "5. Choosing a course the rules accept"),
    ("p", "The Student visa is not a general permission to study anything. It is permission to study a specific course, at a specific sponsor, at a specific level. Getting this wrong is the most expensive mistake in the process, because it usually surfaces after you have paid a deposit."),
    ("h2", "The course must be with a licensed student sponsor"),
    ("p", "Your education provider must hold a student sponsor licence. The register of licensed sponsors is published by the Home Office and updated regularly - search the provider's name on gov.uk before you apply, not after. A provider that lost its licence mid-year can leave students needing to find a new sponsor, and the visa does not transfer automatically."),
    ("h2", "The level matters for two reasons"),
    ("p", "First, work rights: a course at degree level or above (RQF level 6 or higher) allows 20 hours a week in term time; below degree level it is 10 hours. Second, the Graduate Route: it is for graduates of eligible bachelor's, master's and doctoral courses. A short course, a diploma below degree level, or a professional certificate will not get you post-study work permission."),
    ("h2", "What the CAS must say"),
    ("p", "The Confirmation of Acceptance for Studies is the spine of your application. It carries your course, its level, its start and end dates, the tuition fee for your first year, any deposit paid, any scholarship or sponsorship recorded against you, and the provider's sponsor licence number. Every one of those fields is checked against your application form. A mismatch - a course title spelled differently, a fee that does not match your bank evidence - is a refusal reason in its own right."),
    ("h2", "Courses that carry extra conditions"),
    ("b", [
        "Courses in certain science, engineering and technology subjects at postgraduate level may require an ATAS certificate before you can apply for the visa. Chapter 13 covers this.",
        "Courses with a work placement or an industrial year change your visa end date and sometimes your CAS. Ask the university how the placement year is recorded.",
        "Part-time courses: no work rights and no dependants, regardless of level. If someone offers you a part-time master's with work permission, they are wrong.",
        "Pre-sessional English: if you need one, it changes your start date and your CAS. Plan the six months before your main course around it.",
    ]),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 6 ----------------
    ("h1", "6. The university choice now has a visa consequence"),
    ("p", "Until 2026, choosing between two similar universities was mostly about fees, city and ranking. From 1 January 2027 it is also about how long you may stay afterwards, because the government has said the two-year Graduate Route will be kept for graduates of universities that meet new tests of sponsor compliance and graduate labour market outcomes - and 18 months for everyone else."),
    ("p", "That is the announcement. Here is what is not yet known: the definition of 'high compliance', how 'strong labour market outcomes' will be measured, whether the list will be published per university or per course, and what happens to students already enrolled when the criteria land. A written parliamentary answer on 4 November 2026 said only that further details, including transitional arrangements, would be announced in due course."),
    ("h2", "What to do with an undefined rule"),
    ("n", [
        "Do not let an agent tell you a university 'qualifies'. Nobody outside the Home Office knows yet. Ask for the source, in writing, and expect none.",
        "Prefer universities with an unblemished sponsorship record. Compliance is the one half of the test that is already measurable: refusal rates on their CAS, enrolment verification, and whether they have ever had a licence downgraded.",
        "Prefer universities whose graduates visibly get sponsored jobs in your field. If a course's own employment page cannot name employers who sponsor, assume it does not happen there.",
        "Ask the international office directly, by email, what they know about the criteria and whether they expect to qualify. Keep the reply. If the criteria later exclude them, that email is your negotiating position on a deposit refund.",
        "Build your plan so 18 months is enough. If your path to sponsorship needs 24 months, you have built a plan that depends on a rule that has not been written.",
    ]),
    ("h2", "The signals that actually predict a sponsored job"),
    ("t", (
        ["Signal", "Why it matters", "Where to look"],
        [
            ["Employer partnerships listed on the course page", "Placement pipelines are usually formal, not accidental", "Course page, careers service page"],
            ["Accreditation by a professional body", "Some accreditations come with employer networks and exemptions", "Course page, accrediting body register"],
            ["Named alumni destinations with employers", "A list of job titles is weaker than a list of employers", "Course page, LinkedIn alumni filter"],
            ["Careers service that runs UK sponsor-aware advice", "Some services still coach students for jobs that cannot sponsor", "Ask them directly"],
            ["Location with a large employer base in your field", "Commuting distance beats prestige for interview conversion", "Job boards, filtered by city"],
        ],
        [0.30, 0.38, 0.32],
    )),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 7 ----------------
    ("h1", "7. January 2027 or September 2027"),
    ("p", "There are two realistic entry points left in this cycle, and they behave very differently. January 2027 is a small intake with fast deadlines and less competition for places. September 2027 is the main intake, with the full scholarship calendar and the deepest course list. Your Graduate Route outcome is identical for both, because it depends on when you apply for the Graduate visa, not when you started."),
    ("t", (
        ["", "January 2027 intake", "September 2027 intake"],
        [
            ["Course range", "Narrow - mostly business, computing, health, some engineering", "Full range, including courses with placements"],
            ["Application window", "Now. Many courses are closed or closing", "Opens now, peaks January to April 2027"],
            ["CAS issuance", "Often 4-8 weeks before start", "June to August 2027"],
            ["Visa application", "Immediately after CAS - your 28-day clock must be finished before this", "June to August 2027"],
            ["Scholarships", "Thin, and often tied to paying a deposit within days", "Widest - GREAT, Chevening, university awards"],
            ["Living cost figure you must show", "GBP 1,570 / 1,203 (post 30 Nov 2026)", "Same figure unless rules change again"],
            ["Competition for places", "Lower", "Higher"],
        ],
        [0.28, 0.36, 0.36],
    )),
    ("h2", "If you are going in January 2027"),
    ("p", "You are already late, and your sequence is fixed: offer, deposit, CAS, 28 days of funds, application, decision, fly. The 28-day clock is the constraint that ruins January applicants, because the money must be static in an account while the CAS is being issued. Start the 28 days as soon as you know roughly what you will need, using the higher post-30-November figure, and do not move a rupee until you have applied."),
    ("h2", "If you are going in September 2027"),
    ("p", "You have time to do this properly, which mostly means two things: apply early enough to be considered automatically for university scholarships (most are assessed on the application, not on a separate form), and treat the money as a project with a calendar rather than a scramble in July. The scholarship deadlines in Chapter 27 are the ones to diarise - several fall in March and May 2027, months before your visa."),
    ("call", ("The one date that beats all others", "If you will finish your course before 31 December 2026 - for example on a course that started in January 2026 - apply for the Graduate visa as soon as your university confirms completion to the Home Office. You do not need to wait for graduation or a certificate. Applying before 1 January 2027 gives you two years instead of 18 months, and that is worth more than any scholarship you will find in this book.")),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 8 ----------------
    ("h1", "8. Money rule one: the 28-day clock"),
    ("p", "This single rule refuses more Indian applications than any other. It is also the easiest to get right, because it is arithmetic and a calendar."),
    ("h2", "The rule, exactly"),
    ("b", [
        "You must show enough money to cover your course fee for one academic year (up to nine months, as stated on your CAS) plus your living costs for up to nine months.",
        "The money must have been held for 28 consecutive days.",
        "The 28-day period is counted backwards from the closing balance on the most recent bank statement or letter you provide.",
        "That 28-day period must end no more than 31 days before the date you apply.",
        "The evidence itself must be dated no more than 31 days before you apply.",
        "The balance must not dip below the required figure on any day in that window - not on the last day, on any day.",
    ]),
    ("h2", "The example the Home Office gives, and what it means for you"),
    ("p", "If you submit your application on 1 January, you must show the funds were held for at least the 28-day period ending on 1 December. Work backwards from your intended application date, not forwards from today. If you want to apply on 15 January 2027, the 28-day window can end anywhere from 15 December 2026 to 14 January 2027 - but the statement you submit must be dated within 31 days of 15 January, so in practice you submit a statement dated late December or early January."),
    ("t", (
        ["You plan to apply on", "Earliest safe day to start holding the full amount", "Statement you submit"],
        [
            ["15 Dec 2026", "1 Nov 2026", "Dated 14 Nov - 15 Dec 2026"],
            ["10 Jan 2027", "1 Dec 2026", "Dated 10 Dec 2026 - 10 Jan 2027"],
            ["1 Mar 2027", "20 Jan 2027", "Dated 29 Jan - 1 Mar 2027"],
            ["15 Jun 2027", "5 May 2027", "Dated 15 May - 15 Jun 2027"],
            ["1 Aug 2027", "20 Jun 2027", "Dated 1 Jul - 1 Aug 2027"],
        ],
        [0.26, 0.40, 0.34],
    )),
    ("p", "Those dates are deliberately conservative - they assume the 28-day period ends right at the start of the permitted window, which is the safe way to plan. Confirm the exact rule text on the Home Office financial evidence page before you apply, because the two 31-day windows interact and a mistake here is not appealable on the ground that you misunderstood it."),
    ("h2", "The four ways people break the clock"),
    ("n", [
        "Moving money in. A top-up on day 20 resets nothing officially, but it creates a statement showing a lower opening balance, and a caseworker reading the lowest balance in the period sees a shortfall.",
        "Paying the deposit from the same account. Perfectly normal, but it drops the balance. Either hold the deposit separately or hold enough to cover the fee net of deposit.",
        "Using a fixed deposit that matures mid-window. A maturing FD can be acceptable, but the evidence must show the money available; a certificate with a maturity date after your application is a problem.",
        "Submitting a statement generated a week after the money arrived, with no closing balance history. The statement must show the balance over the period, not just today's number.",
    ]),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 9 ----------------
    ("h1", "9. Money rule two: what counts, and what does not"),
    ("p", "The Home Office publishes an explicit list of acceptable evidence and an explicit list of things that are not acceptable. The second list is where people lose a year."),
    ("h2", "Accepted"),
    ("b", [
        "Your own money, in an account you control, at a bank regulated by the financial regulator of the country it operates in.",
        "Your parent's money, in an account they control, with a letter confirming they agree to you using it for your studies - plus evidence of the relationship where the surname differs or the account is not obviously theirs.",
        "Your partner's money, if your partner is in the UK or applying at the same time.",
        "A student loan from a government, a government-sponsored loan company, or a regulated or academic student loans scheme, with a loan letter meeting the stated conditions.",
        "Official financial sponsorship - a government, an international organisation, a university - recorded on your CAS, or confirmed in a sponsor letter with contact details and amounts.",
        "Evidence types: bank statements (paper or electronic download), building society passbooks, certificates of deposit, or a letter from the bank.",
    ]),
    ("h2", "Not accepted"),
    ("b", [
        "Overdrafts, and any account shown in debit.",
        "Cryptocurrency holdings.",
        "Stocks, shares and mutual fund units.",
        "Pension funds.",
        "Accounts at institutions that are not regulated by the financial regulator in the country where they operate.",
        "Accounts that do not keep electronic records.",
        "Money you cannot show was acquired lawfully.",
    ]),
    ("p", "Two Indian-specific traps. First, a demat or mutual fund statement showing a healthy portfolio is worth nothing for this purpose - it is not cash in a regulated deposit account. Second, a co-operative credit society or a chit fund is not a bank for these purposes unless it is regulated as one; if in doubt, move the money to a scheduled commercial bank well before your 28-day window and keep it there."),
    ("h2", "The bank letter that works"),
    ("p", "If you use a bank letter instead of statements, it must be on letterhead, dated within 31 days of your application, and show your name, the bank's name, and the balance - plus, in practice, the account number and the currency. Ask the branch for a 'balance confirmation letter for UK visa purposes' and check it states the amount in pounds or clearly in rupees with the account currency named. A letter that says 'sufficient balance' without a number is refused."),
    ("call", ("The verification risk", "The Home Office says it may verify your evidence with your bank, and that if it cannot verify it, your application may be refused. That means: use a bank with a functioning branch and a real email address, keep the relationship manager's contact details, and never use an account opened through an agent who 'arranged' the funds.")),
    ("pb", None),

    # ---------------- Chapter 10 ----------------
    ("h1", "10. Money rule three: parents, loans and deposits"),
    ("h2", "Using a parent's account"),
    ("p", "This is normal and allowed. What is required is a letter from the parent confirming they consent to you using the money for your studies, plus evidence that they are your parent. Where the surname matches and the documents line up, that is usually a passport or birth certificate. Where it does not - a mother's maiden name on the account, a name change after marriage, an adopted child - you need the document that proves the link: birth certificate, adoption certificate, or a court document for a legal guardian."),
    ("p", "Write the letter in one page, dated, signed, with the parent's contact details, the amount, the account number, and a sentence saying the money is available to you for tuition and living costs and will not be withdrawn. Do not write a letter that promises to 'arrange funds as required' - the requirement is that the money is already there."),
    ("h2", "Education loans"),
    ("p", "A loan can count, but the rules are narrower than most students assume. The acceptable route is a student loan from a government, a government-sponsored loan company, or a regulated or academic student loans scheme, evidenced by a loan letter that is dated within six months of your application, confirms the loan is a student loan from such a provider, confirms there are no conditions on release other than a successful application to study, confirms the amount and that the loan is for you, and confirms the money will be available to you or paid to your sponsor before your course starts."),
    ("p", "The practical consequence for Indian students: a sanctioned-but-undisbursed private loan letter is not, on its own, the same as money in an account. The safest structure is a disbursement into your own account before your 28-day window starts, so that the evidence you submit is a bank statement showing the balance - the strongest form of evidence there is. If you are relying on the loan letter instead, check every one of the bullet points above against the letter before you submit, and get the lender to reissue it if any is missing."),
    ("h2", "The accommodation deposit offset"),
    ("p", "If you have paid a deposit for accommodation arranged by your sponsor, that deposit can be offset against the maintenance figure, up to a cap - GBP 1,570 for applications on or after 30 November 2026, up from GBP 1,529. This is genuinely useful: a GBP 1,000 hall deposit reduces what you must show by GBP 1,000, provided the CAS or the university's confirmation shows the deposit and the amount."),
    ("p", "It only works for accommodation arranged by the sponsor. A deposit paid to a private landlord for a flat you found on a Facebook group is not offset, and paying one before you have a visa is a common way to lose money. If you must secure private housing early, use a provider with a written no-visa refund policy and keep the clause."),
    ("h2", "What to do in the week before you apply"),
    ("n", [
        "Download the statement that covers your whole 28-day window, plus the balance on the day before you apply.",
        "Check the lowest balance in the window, not the closing balance. If it dips, wait and restart the clock.",
        "Check the date on every document against the 31-day rule.",
        "Check that the name on the bank account matches your passport exactly, including middle names.",
        "Check the CAS fee against the statement arithmetic - fee minus deposit paid, plus living costs, must be covered.",
        "Do not move money, pay fees, or accept a refund into that account until the decision arrives.",
    ]),
    ("pb", None),
]
